CORE
main.cpp

^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ member as lvalue: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ member template: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The resolver built `cpp-this->m' for the member without marking it an
lvalue.  libc++'s __perfect_forward_impl::operator() names its tuple member
in its trailing return type this way.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this
program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
